  "narrative": "The RaMP enrichment analysis returned five top-ranked pathways, all with FDR = 0.0. The most relevant pathway for the observed metabolite cluster is **Androgen and Estrogen Metabolism** (KEGG map00150; also annotated in SMP00068 and Reactome R-HSA-196071), with six matched input compounds: dehydroepiandrosterone (C01227), androstenedione (C00280), testosterone (C00535), dihydrotestosterone (C03917), estrone (C00468), and estradiol (C00951). A second closely related entry, *Metabolism of steroid hormones* (Reactome R-HSA-196071), shares the same six steroids and reflects the same biological theme.\n\nMembership verification confirmed that testosterone (C00535), androstenedione (C00280), and dihydrotestosterone (C03917) each map to the KEGG and SMPDB Androgen and Estrogen Metabolism pathways, as well as to Reactome steroid-hormone biosynthesis modules. The KEGG reaction graph also confirms a direct, reversible relationship between androstenedione and testosterone (path length = 1 hop), placing them in immediate upstream–downstream relationship. The six matched steroids form a coherent biosynthetic gradient from the C19 androgen precursors (DHEA → androstenedione → testosterone → DHT) to the C18 aromatized estrogens (estrone ↔ estradiol).\n\nThe non-steroidal compounds — caffeine (C07481), inosine 2',3'-cyclic phosphate (C05768), N-hydroxy-4-aminobiphenyl (C20319), 4-hydroxy-L-glutamic acid (C05947), and sucrose (C00089) — did not contribute to the steroid pathway signal. Their enrichment status was not significant in the top results; caffeine may engage xenobiotic metabolism pathways, and the cyclic nucleotide and aromatic amine could reflect oxidative stress or DNA-damage responses, but these require separate investigation. The primary biological story is therefore a marked perturbation of the **Androgen and Estrogen Metabolism** pathway, driven collectively by six sex-steroid intermediates, consistent with altered steroidogenic enzyme activity (e.g., aromatase, 17β-HSD, or 5α-reductase) in the treatment condition.",
